AB  - Colombia ranks 10th as one of the largest producers of cocoa worldwide, which along with other tropical crops is of great importance in the agricultural and economic sector for its numerous organoleptic and medicinal characteristics; However, these crops have been affected due to poor agricultural practices, which has contributed to the appearance and expansion of diseases caused by fungi, among which the Witch Broom, generated by Moniliophtora perniciosa, has been one of the more incidents in the country. The objective of this work was to offer an alternative to this problem, contributing in the search of phytosanitary agents for the control of M. perniciosa from extracts obtained from species of the Piper genus, considered a potential genus with antifungal activity. To this end, isolation and purification was carried out in several culture media (PDA, PDA ½, Malt Extract, oats, V8 and Carrot) of fungal morphotypes associated with the disease from plant samples with symptoms collected in the municipality of Otanche , Boyaca. We also sought to determine the temperature and culture medium that stimulated the growth and conidiogenesis of the possible isolation of M. perniciosa and an isolation of M. roreri evaluating the development of the colonies during 34 days. In spite of not achieving conidium production, we chose to perform the mycelial growth inhibition test by agar dilution method in 12-well boxes with M. roreri, this was done by evaluating 11 Piper species within which They highlighted P. asperiusculum, P. grande, P. statarium, P. artanthe and P. nigrum for inhibiting the growth of the pathogen in percentages higher than 50%, which showed the great potential and biological properties of these species as substances antifungal.
