AB  - Moniliophthora roreri is a hemibiotrophic fungus and the causal agent of cacao moniliasis, a major disease affecting cacao production in the Americas. Here, we functionally characterized Mr BASIDIN3 ( Mr B3), a novel candidate effector identified from transcripts differentially expressed in M. roreri spores incubated in cacao extract. Mr B3 encodes a 256-amino-acid protein with a predicted signal peptide, no transmembrane regions, and features consistent with an apoplastic effector. The protein lacks known conserved domains, and structural analyses predicted a predominantly α-helical structure. Recombinant r Mr B3 was heterologously expressed in Escherichia coli , purified, and validated by mass spectrometry. The recombinant protein exhibited high thermal stability and induced necrosis and wilting in Solanum lycopersicum leaves, accompanied by increased H₂O₂ accumulation. r Mr B3 also elicited differential responses among the Theobroma cacao genotypes. Affinity capture followed by LC–MS/MS identified an LRR-containing protein from T. cacao (TcLRR) as a potential host-associated protein. Molecular docking supported a structurally favorable association between MrB3 and TcLRR. Collectively, these results characterize Mr B3 as a novel candidate effector from M. roreri with biological activity and features consistent with a secreted effector. The identification of TcLRR as a potential host molecular target provides a basis for investigating the molecular mechanisms underlying M. roreri–T. cacao interaction and the role of effector-mediated modulation of plant defense.
